NNOOTTEESS
       __FFIILLEE__OOFFFFSSEETT__BBIITTSS should be defined to  be  64  in  code  that  uses  a
       pointer  to rreeaaddaahheeaadd, if the code is intended to be portable to tradi‐
       tional 32‐bit x86 and ARM platforms where ooffff__tt’s width defaults to  32
       bits.

BBUUGGSS
       rreeaaddaahheeaadd() attempts to schedule the reads in the background and return
       immediately.  However, it may block while it reads the filesystem meta‐
       data  needed  to  locate  the requested blocks.  This occurs frequently
       with ext[234] on large files using indirect blocks instead of  extents,
       giving the appearance that the call blocks until the requested data has
       been read.

